    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `StandardDomain.clear_doc`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `clear_doc` function is a method of the `StandardDomain` class, designed to remove all references to a specific document from several internal data structures. This is useful when a document is being removed or replaced, ensuring that no stale references remain. The function takes a single parameter, `docname`, which is a string representing the name of the document to be cleared. It returns `None`, indicating that it performs an action rather than returning a value.\n\nThe function works by iterating over several dictionaries (`progoptions`, `objects`, `_terms`, `labels`, and `anonlabels`) that store information about various elements associated with documents. For each dictionary, it creates a list of keys (`to_remove1`, `to_remove2`, etc.) where the document name matches the `docname` parameter. It then deletes these keys from their respective dictionaries, effectively removing all references to the specified document.\n\nThe function does not rely on any external helper functions or methods; it operates directly on the instance's attributes. It does not handle any specific errors or edge cases, such as missing keys or invalid document names, as these are assumed to be managed elsewhere in the code. The primary side effect of this function is the modification of the internal state of the `StandardDomain` instance by removing document references.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, retu